---
run: round-trip
fixpoint: executed
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-nested-dot-resolves-against-cwd — QUESTION: a sourced file's OWN relative `.` — is it
# resolved against the current working directory at that moment, or against the directory the
# sourcing file lives in?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it: `rul-dot-resolves-as-sh` [TYPED] rules that a relative operand
# resolves against the cwd at that load position, and explicitly REJECTS the sourcing-file-relative
# rule on the grounds that it would give the same authored line a different referent under Dorc and
# under stock sh. That rejection is the load model's foundation and was argued from the standard
# rather than measured.
#
# The measurement needs the two candidate directories to differ, and the floor sandbox holds no
# subdirectories (`gap-no-subdirectory-fixtures`), so the difference is made by MOVING the cwd:
# `mid.sh` does `cd /` and only then sources its sibling by a relative name.
#
# ANSWERED, and the shells AGREE on the mechanic — they differ only in the status of a construct
# they both rejected, which is the one thing `fence-rejection-rc` says no dialect rule may depend
# on. Gate-9 cannot tell those two kinds of difference apart, so this case carries NO
# `expected.emitted` section; re-arming it is one line, and would go red on a status nothing is
# entitled to read.
#
# measured-divergence: (mise run test:floor -- floor30-atlas, dash 0.5.12 ∩ posh 0.14.1, WSL)
#   dash: mid-before-cd
#         mid-after-cd
#         book: contained status=[2]
#         book: leaf-mark-in-book=[unset]
#         book-tail
#   posh: mid-before-cd
#         mid-after-cd
#         book: contained status=[1]
#         book: leaf-mark-in-book=[unset]
#         book-tail
#   MEANING: the ONLY differing byte is the rejected `.`'s exit status (2 vs 1). Everything that
#   answers the question is identical: after `cd /`, `. ./leaf.sh` does NOT find the sibling in
#   either shell, so `.` resolves against the CWD and not against the sourcing file's directory.
#   `rul-dot-resolves-as-sh` is confirmed, and the divergence is `fence-rejection-rc` material.
#   SECOND MEASUREMENT, unplanned and load-bearing: neither shell ever printed mid.sh's own
#   `mid: relative-load-after-cd-failed` line, and mid.sh's tail is gone in both — so the failed
#   `.` ended the sourced file even though it sat as the LEFT OPERAND of `||`. A failing special
#   builtin is not catchable there. `floor30-atlas-dot-missing-file-is-fatal` measures that head-on.
( . ./mid.sh ) || printf 'book: contained status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'book: leaf-mark-in-book=[%s]\n' "${leaf_mark-unset}"
printf 'book-tail\n'

-- mid.sh --
printf 'mid-before-cd\n'
cd /
printf 'mid-after-cd\n'
. ./leaf.sh || printf 'mid: relative-load-after-cd-failed status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'mid: leaf-mark=[%s]\n' "${leaf_mark-unset}"

-- leaf.sh --
leaf_mark=loaded
printf 'leaf-ran\n'

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=e4c55d3cf2448b6e7141db0ce880b71471bdf6e32026386297f8ed81049d3963 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

